\section{Model}{

Let \eqn{a_{ij} = 1} when product \eqn{j} reaches respondent \eqn{i}, and
let \eqn{r_j = \sum_i a_{ij}} be the individual reach of product \eqn{j}.
The binary variable \eqn{x_j} selects product \eqn{j}. The continuous
variable \eqn{z_i \ge 0} counts respondent \eqn{i} as not reached. The
constraints are
\deqn{z_i + \sum_j a_{ij} x_j \ge 1 \quad \textrm{for each respondent } i,}
\deqn{\sum_j x_j = k.}
For integer \eqn{x}, the smallest feasible \eqn{z_i} is 0 when a selected
product reaches respondent \eqn{i} and 1 when none does. Only the
product variables need to be integer, which keeps the solve fast.

The stages solve in sequence. Each later stage keeps the earlier criteria at
their optimal values.
\itemize{
\item Reach: minimize \eqn{\sum_i z_i}, which maximizes the number of
respondents reached.
\item \code{"frequency"}: maximize \eqn{\sum_j r_j x_j}, the total number of
product and respondent pairs where the product reaches the respondent.
\item \code{"penetration"}: minimize \eqn{\sum_j x_j / r_j}, which maximizes the
harmonic mean of the individual reaches of the selected products. When
reach and frequency are fixed, this prefers portfolios whose products
have similar individual reach.
}

If portfolios still tie after the last stage, the solver returns one of
them.

With \code{fixed}, every portfolio considered contains the fixed products, so
the stages choose only the other \code{size - length(fixed)} products. A row
\eqn{\sum_{j \in F} x_j = |F|} adds this to the model.
}

\section{Limits and warnings}{

The penetration search examines at most \code{getOption("turfLP.max_pool", 1000)}
additional portfolios, with a budget of
\code{getOption("turfLP.max_pool_seconds", 30)} seconds. Use a nonnegative whole
number for the portfolio limit and a nonnegative number of seconds (\code{Inf}
for no time limit). For example, \code{options(turfLP.max_pool_seconds = 120)}
changes the budget for subsequent calls; restore the previous option after use.

These are not whole-call timeouts. The reach stage, a frequency stage before
penetration, and the first penetration solve have no time limit. If the pool
search stops early and frequency follows penetration, its recovery search
gets a separate budget of the same length.

A limit produces an R warning that names the criterion whose optimum was
not proved. The function still returns the best portfolio found; reach stays
optimal. Keep the warning with the result. Warnings are not stored in the
returned list. Input errors or failed solves stop with an error instead.
